{"qid":"Q217","names":["Republic of Moldova"],"title":"Republic of Moldova","endonym":{"native":"Moldova","lang":"ro"},"capsule":"Moldova declared independence on 27 August 1991 as the Soviet Union dissolved, succeeding the Moldavian Soviet Socialist Republic; formal international recognition followed rapidly, and the country joined the United Nations in 1992. A brief but destructive war with the Russian-backed Transnistrian separatists in 1992 left the eastern strip of the country under de facto Transnistrian control, a frozen conflict that persists. Moldova is one of Europe's poorest states and has pursued EU association since the 2014 Association Agreement; it received EU candidate status in June 2022 and opened accession negotiations in 2024.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Moldova's internationally recognized borders (excluding Transnistria) are precisely defined; the effective administrative boundary with the unrecognized Pridnestrovian Moldavian Republic is well documented but not internationally formalized.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Republic of Moldova","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Moldova"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Transnistria War","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transnistria_War"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Moldova–European Union relations","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moldova%E2%80%93European_Union_relations"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Dissolution of the Soviet Union","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dissolution_of_the_Soviet_Union"}],"britannica":"place/Moldova","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Moldova","rulers":[{"qid":"Q347679","name":"Mircea Snegur"